{{Short description|Naming conventions of Sinchew}}
'''Sinchewese naming conventions''' are the personal naming practices used in [[Sinchew]], distinct from the naming traditions of the [[Kingdom of Zong]] from which the Sinchewese population originated. The central feature of the system is the use of a [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] [[dharma name]] (法名, huat-miâ) as the primary personal name, assigned during a coming-of-age ceremony conducted by a Baiduist monk. The system emerged from the colonial experience of the Baiduist community in Sinchew and was formalised as a civic institution following the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] (1726–1733).

== Structure ==

A Sinchewese personal name consists of two elements: a surname (姓, sèⁿ) retained from the Zong family tradition, and a dharma name (法名, huat-miâ) assigned during the [[#Ocean Crossing Ceremony|Ocean Crossing Ceremony]]. The surname precedes the dharma name, following the same order as Zong naming conventions. For example, in the name Lîm Huat-tiat (林法哲), Lîm (林) is the surname and Huat-tiat (法哲) is the dharma name.

Sinchewese surnames are identical to those of Zong, reflecting the origin of the Sinchewese population as settlers expelled from the eastern coast of Zong beginning in 1606. The most common surnames include Tân (陳), Lîm (林), Ong (王), Ng (黃), and Chiu (周). Sinchewese pronunciation of these surnames has diverged slightly from Zong Hokkien due to approximately two centuries of colonial isolation, but the written characters and romanisation conventions remain the same.

=== Birth names ===

Before receiving a dharma name, a child is given a birth name (生名, seⁿ-miâ) by the parents at birth. Birth names are typically one or two characters and follow no prescribed format. After the Ocean Crossing Ceremony, the dharma name becomes the individual's primary name in all public and official contexts. The birth name is retained for use within the family, particularly by parents and grandparents. Using an individual's birth name outside the family circle is considered overly familiar or disrespectful, as it presumes an intimacy that the speaker may not possess.

Birth names are not recorded in official documents. The monastery registry, maintained by the Baiduist monastery that conducted the Ocean Crossing Ceremony, serves as the authoritative record of an individual's legal name. This registry system has functioned as the civil registration mechanism in Sinchew since the formalisation of the naming system after independence.

== Ocean Crossing Ceremony ==

The Ocean Crossing Ceremony (海渡式, Hái-tō͘-sik) is the coming-of-age ceremony at which a child receives their dharma name. The ceremony is named after the Ocean Crossing (海渡), the central [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] teaching that likens spiritual practice to a voyage across the sea. The name reflects the symbolic parallel between the child's transition from childhood to adulthood and the Baiduist voyage of spiritual development.

The ceremony takes place at age twelve. It is conducted by a Baiduist monk at the local monastery and is attended by the child's family and community members. During the ceremony, the monk meditates on the child's character, family history, and Baiduist principles, then selects a dharma name. The name is composed of two characters: one drawn from the Baiduist lexicon and one personal character chosen by the monk to reflect the individual's nature or the family's circumstances.

The name is recorded in the monastery registry, which serves as the official civic record. After the ceremony, the child is regarded as an adult member of the community, and the dharma name is used in all subsequent official and social contexts.

== Dharma name vocabulary ==

Dharma names are constructed from a lexicon of characters drawn from [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] teachings and imagery. The lexicon is organised into five thematic categories, though in practice many names draw from multiple categories, and some characters carry meanings that span several.

{| class="wikitable"
! Category !! Characters !! Meanings
|-
| Ocean and Crossing || 海 (hái), 渡 (tō͘), 波 (po), 潮 (tiâu), 岸 (hng), 帆 (phâng) || sea, crossing, wave, tide, shore, sail
|-
| Enlightenment and Awakening || 覺 (kak), 悟 (ngō), 明 (bêng), 智 (tì), 真 (chin) || awakening, realisation, clarity, wisdom, truth
|-
| Compassion and Virtue || 慈 (chû), 善 (siān), 悲 (pi), 淨 (chiāⁿ), 德 (tek) || compassion, goodness, empathy, purity, virtue
|-
| Interconnection || 結 (ket), 連 (liân), 緣 (iân) || knot, connection, affinity
|-
| Dharma and Path || 法 (huat), 道 (tō), 行 (hēng), 心 (sim) || dharma, way, practice, heart
|}

A typical dharma name pairs one character from this lexicon with a second character chosen by the assigning monk. The second character may be drawn from the same lexicon or from the broader vocabulary of classical Chinese, provided that the resulting name is consistent with Baiduist principles. For example, the name Huat-tiat (法哲) pairs 法 (dharma) with 哲 (wisdom, philosopher), while the name Hái-kong (海光) pairs 海 (ocean) with 光 (light, radiance).

Not all dharma names contain characters that are immediately recognisable as Baiduist. Characters such as 德 (virtue), 明 (clarity), and 真 (truth) belong to both the Baiduist lexicon and the broader Chinese naming tradition, and names using these characters may not be distinguishable from Zong names in isolation. The distinction lies in the ceremony and the institutional framework rather than in every individual name.

== Historical development ==

The dharma name system emerged from the specific conditions of the Baiduist community in Sinchew during the colonial period.

=== Monastic community and early practice (1606–1680s) ===

Following the [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong]] (1606–1650), over one million Zong Baiduists were transported to the Sinchew colony at [[Tamsui]]. Baiduist monks were among the expelled, and the monasteries they established in the colony became the primary institutions of community organisation. The monks operated schools, provided charity and medical care, maintained literacy and numeracy education, and offered mutual aid that the Tianist colonial administration had ceased to provide. In this environment, the monasteries acquired a civic authority that extended beyond the religious sphere.

During this early period, monks began giving dharma names to lay children as a form of blessing. In Zong, dharma names had been reserved for ordained monks who had taken vows. The extension of dharma names to lay children was an innovation specific to the Sinchewese colonial context, reflecting the central role of the monastery in community life. Initially, these dharma names were supplementary — children possessed both a traditional secular name and a dharma name, with the secular name remaining primary.

=== Expansion and transition (1680s–1720s) ===

Over the following generations, the use of dharma names as primary names became increasingly common, particularly among families with strong monastic ties. The [[Sinchew War]] (1648–1656) and the continuing isolation from Zong accelerated the cultural divergence. By the early eighteenth century, the practice was widespread but not yet universal. Records from the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716) show a mixture of naming practices: some figures, such as Free Sinchew council speaker [[Tân Huat-seng]] (陳法生), bore dharma names, while others, such as Sinchew governor [[Tân Bûn-seng]] (陳文成) and general Tan Tek-hui (陳德輝), retained traditional Zong-style names. This transitional period reflects the uneven adoption of the system across different segments of Sinchewese society.

=== Formalisation (1733 onward) ===

Following the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] (1726–1733) and the establishment of the Republic of Sinchew, the dharma name system was formalised as a civic institution. The Ocean Crossing Ceremony was established as the standard coming-of-age ritual, and the monastery registry was recognised as the official civil record. The flag of the republic, designed by Baiduist monk and revolutionary intellectual [[Lîm Huat-tiat]] (林法哲), incorporated the Baiduist knot — a symbol that had been used in revolutionary insignia and that exemplified the integration of Baiduist religious heritage into the civic identity of the new republic.

The formalisation of the system did not abolish traditional naming practices entirely. Families that had retained Zong-style secular names were not required to adopt dharma names retroactively, and the system coexisted with older naming practices for several generations before becoming near-universal among the Baiduist population.

== Non-Baiduist participation ==

The Republic of Sinchew is a secular state, and the Ocean Crossing Ceremony is available to non-Baiduists on an optional basis. Non-Baiduist families may choose to have their children participate in the ceremony and receive a dharma name, in which case the ceremony is conducted in a civic rather than a strictly religious manner. Non-Baiduists who do not participate use traditional Zong-style names or follow their own cultural naming conventions.

The optional nature of the ceremony reflects the constitutional principle that the state recognises Baiduist cultural heritage while maintaining a neutral posture toward all faiths. Non-Baiduists are not disadvantaged by not holding a dharma name, as the civil registration system accommodates both dharma names and traditional names.

